WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.584 --> 00:00:10.083
By 2050, the world's population is expected
to soar to almost 10 billion people...

00:00:10.292 --> 00:00:12.834
...and two-thirds of us will live in cities

00:00:13.792 --> 00:00:15.334
Space will be at a premium

00:00:16.834 --> 00:00:18.334
High-rise offers a solution

00:00:18.709 --> 00:00:22.250
But concrete and steel, the materials
we currently use to build high...

00:00:22.375 --> 00:00:24.916
...have a large carbon footprint

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:28.667
An answer might lie in a natural material
we've used for millennia

00:00:30.167 --> 00:00:33.751
Our view is that all buildings should
be made of timber

00:00:34.375 --> 00:00:37.999
We think that we should be looking at
concrete and steel...

00:00:38.876 --> 00:00:41.250
...like we look at petrol and diesel

00:00:42.125 --> 00:00:45.709
I think it's very realistic to think that
someone will build a wooden skyscraper...

00:00:45.876 --> 00:00:48.792
...in the coming years, there's a lot of
potential that's unrealised...

00:00:48.918 --> 00:00:51.918
...for using timber at a very large scale

00:00:54.334 --> 00:00:57.334
Throughout history, buildings have
been made of wood

00:00:57.626 --> 00:01:01.167
But it has one major drawback,
it acts as kindling

00:01:01.876 --> 00:01:05.999
Fire has destroyed large swathes
of some of the world's great cities

00:01:07.417 --> 00:01:11.999
But by the early twentieth century, the era
of modern steelmaking had arrived

00:01:12.083 --> 00:01:17.709
Steel was strong, could be moulded into
any shape and used to reinforce concrete

00:01:17.999 --> 00:01:21.292
It allowed architects to build higher
than ever before

00:01:22.334 --> 00:01:25.417
So why, after more than a century of
concrete and steel...

00:01:25.542 --> 00:01:29.083
...are some architects proposing
a return to wood?

00:01:29.834 --> 00:01:33.334
If concrete were to arrive as a new material
on Dragon's Den...

00:01:33.501 --> 00:01:36.167
...if you were to pitch it and say, "I've got
this brand-new material...

00:01:36.584 --> 00:01:40.209
...it's liquid, and you can pour it
into any shape and it'll solidify"

00:01:40.834 --> 00:01:42.042
That sounds great

00:01:42.292 --> 00:01:47.083
But when you say, "We need a whole
new fleet of trucks to move it around

00:01:47.209 --> 00:01:49.125
And actually, when it solidifies,
it's not strong enough

00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:51.542
We have to stick this other stuff
in it called steel"

00:01:51.876 --> 00:01:53.876
I don't think it would be a compelling case

00:01:56.584 --> 00:02:00.958
Concrete and steel are costly to produce
and heavy to transport

00:02:01.042 --> 00:02:04.709
Wood, however, can be grown sustainably
and it's lighter than concrete

00:02:05.083 --> 00:02:06.999
And crucially, as trees grow...

00:02:07.167 --> 00:02:11.918
...they absorb carbon dioxide from the air,
locking it into the timber

00:02:12.083 --> 00:02:16.667
One study showed that using wood to
construct a 125-metre skyscraper...

00:02:16.834 --> 00:02:21.042
...could reduce the building's carbon
footprint by up to 75%

00:02:23.292 --> 00:02:26.459
Regular timber isn't malleable like steel or
concrete...

00:02:26.626 --> 00:02:28.834
...and isn't strong enough
to build high

00:02:28.959 --> 00:02:31.584
But engineers have come up with a solution

00:02:31.709 --> 00:02:35.918
It's called cross-laminated timber,
or CLT for short

00:02:36.292 --> 00:02:40.250
It's basically a new material, even though
the underlying material...

00:02:40.375 --> 00:02:42.834
...is something we've used for millennia

00:02:43.292 --> 00:02:48.709
It's cross-laminated so the layers of wood
are glued at 90 degrees to each other

00:02:48.876 --> 00:02:52.626
That makes for a very, very stable material

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:57.417
CLT is light and it's comparable in
strength to concrete and steel

00:02:58.167 --> 00:03:02.125
But how does it cope when burnt
with a high heat source?

00:03:02.792 --> 00:03:05.125
Charred wood is extremely insulating

00:03:05.584 --> 00:03:08.083
That's the tree's natural protection
against a forest fire

00:03:09.626 --> 00:03:15.875
It chars, it loses some of its structural mass,
but when you remove the source of flame...

00:03:15.959 --> 00:03:18.250
...it extinguishes itself

00:03:19.083 --> 00:03:22.125
When steel gets hot, it gets a bit softer

00:03:22.459 --> 00:03:25.458
We've actually seen some steel roofs
collapse in fires...

00:03:25.542 --> 00:03:27.542
...where wooden roofs have not

00:03:30.459 --> 00:03:32.292
London architects Waugh Thistleton...

00:03:32.417 --> 00:03:35.709
...are already designing buildings with
this new kind of timber

00:03:37.918 --> 00:03:41.542
There's a CLT building behind, where the
timber building sits behind timber clad

00:03:41.876 --> 00:03:45.751
And then there's a really simple
galvanised steel walkway

00:03:46.834 --> 00:03:49.834
Cross laminated timber is a material we
work with a lot

00:03:49.999 --> 00:03:51.709
Once these panels arrive on site...

00:03:51.834 --> 00:03:54.417
...we're building a floor a week, at least

00:03:54.751 --> 00:03:58.751
So this is incredibly fast, maybe twice as
fast as concrete

00:03:58.876 --> 00:04:01.542
Because when you a concrete building -
what we call concrete buildings...

00:04:01.626 --> 00:04:03.999
...are actually floor slabs and columns

00:04:04.375 --> 00:04:07.999
When we build a cross laminated
timber building, it's building floor slabs...

00:04:08.083 --> 00:04:11.542
...all the external walls, all
the internal walls, the lift cores...

00:04:11.667 --> 00:04:14.916
...the stairs, the stair cores,
everything is made of timber

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:17.292
So these are like honeycomb structures

00:04:17.667 --> 00:04:19.333
Andrew and his colleagues designed...

00:04:19.417 --> 00:04:21.959
...Britain's first high-rise wooden
apartment block...

00:04:22.209 --> 00:04:25.751
...and have recently completed the world's
largest timber-based building

00:04:26.626 --> 00:04:28.959
Behind these bricks is a timber core...

00:04:29.083 --> 00:04:33.292
...made from more than 2000 trees,
sourced from sustainable forests

00:04:34.042 --> 00:04:38.626
And this London practice is not alone in
advocating the use of CLT

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:43.125
Ambitious wooden high-rise buildings are
also being constructed in Scandinavia...

00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:45.375
...central Europe and North America

00:04:46.417 --> 00:04:50.999
As yet, nobody has used CLT to build
beyond 55 metres

00:04:52.501 --> 00:04:54.834
But Michael Ramage's research centre in
Cambridge...

00:04:54.959 --> 00:04:56.999
...working with another London practice...

00:04:57.083 --> 00:05:01.876
...has proposed a concept design of a
300-metre tower, to be built on top...

00:05:01.999 --> 00:05:05.959
...of one of London's most iconic concrete
structures - the Barbican

00:05:07.751 --> 00:05:12.417
The way we've engineered the Oakwood
Tower is to look at the global structure

00:05:12.501 --> 00:05:14.459
Is it stable, and would it stand up?

00:05:14.918 --> 00:05:16.250
We believe the answer is yes

00:05:16.375 --> 00:05:22.542
The columns at the base of the Oakwood
Tower would be about 2.5 metres square...

00:05:22.626 --> 00:05:26.876
...so that's solid timber, made of small
elements glued together

00:05:27.542 --> 00:05:32.375
I think we'll probably see incremental
increases from the current height...

00:05:32.751 --> 00:05:35.708
...of about 50 metres, and at some point...

00:05:35.792 --> 00:05:39.501
...someone will make a step change,
probably to up about 100 metres

00:05:40.999 --> 00:05:43.918
Making that jump in height will
be a difficult sell

00:05:44.334 --> 00:05:47.209
The cost of building wooden skyscrapers
is largely unknown...

00:05:47.501 --> 00:05:49.250
...but those costs could be reduced...

00:05:49.375 --> 00:05:53.000
...by prefabricating large sections
of buildings in factories

00:05:53.709 --> 00:05:55.834
And city-dwellers will need
to be persuaded...

00:05:55.918 --> 00:05:59.167
...that CLT does not burn like
ordinary wood

00:06:00.209 --> 00:06:05.167
As an attractive, natural material, wood is
already popular for use in low buildings

00:06:05.792 --> 00:06:10.000
If planners approve,
it could rise to new heights

